  "segments": [
    {
      "id": "t006703",
      "source": "A map has scale 1:200000. The measured map distance between two points is 8 cm. Rule: 1 cm on the map represents 200000 cm in reality; 100,000 cm = 1 km.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006704",
      "source": "Rules.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006705",
      "source": "Multiply map length by the scale denominator, then convert units. A scale is a ratio, not an added distance.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006706",
      "source": "Task.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006707",
      "source": "What is the real straight-line distance in kilometres?",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006708",
      "source": "Step-by-step solution.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006709",
      "source": "1. Real centimetres=8×200000=1600000. 2. Convert to kilometres: 1600000÷100,000=16.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006710",
      "source": "Answer.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006711",
      "source": "16 km.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006712",
      "source": "Knowledge takeaway.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006713",
      "source": "Scale problems are ratio problems: keep units explicit and conversion becomes mechanical.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006714",
      "source": "Formal model.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006715",
      "source": "Proportion d_real=d_map×denominator, followed by unit conversion.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006716",
      "source": "N2. Coordinates and constraint-based location",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006717",
      "source": "Problem 381. Locating a point by constraints: case 1",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006718",
      "source": "Knowledge context.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006719",
      "source": "Coordinates turn location into numbers. Multiple spatial clues can be treated as simultaneous constraints.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006720",
      "source": "Given facts.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006721",
      "source": "A point P lies on an integer grid with x,y from 0 to 6. It satisfies x+y=4 and x−y=0.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006722",
      "source": "Rules.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006723",
      "source": "A location is valid only if it satisfies every stated equation and inequality at the same time.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006724",
      "source": "Task.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006725",
      "source": "Which coordinate(s) can P occupy? Is the solution unique?",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006726",
      "source": "Step-by-step solution.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006727",
      "source": "1. List pairs satisfying x+y=4. 2. Keep only those also satisfying x−y=0. 3. The remaining set is [(2, 2)].",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006728",
      "source": "Answer.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006729",
      "source": "P=(2, 2); the solution is unique.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006730",
      "source": "Knowledge takeaway.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006731",
      "source": "A constraint problem is solved by satisfying all conditions together, not one at a time independently.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006732",
      "source": "Formal model.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006733",
      "source": "Constraint satisfaction over integer pairs (x,y).",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
